<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1712">1712</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Rousseau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Jacques Rousseau was a Genevan-born philosopher, writer and composer whose works shaped the political thought of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-social-contract">The Social Contract</a> (1762) he argued that legitimate government rests on the general will of the people, and in Emile he set out a theory of education. His Confessions and novel Julie influenced the Romantic movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau.jpg" alt="Jean-Jacques Rousseau" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maurice Quentin de La Tour (Life time: 1788) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Quentin_de_La_Tour_-_Portrait_of_Jean-Jacques_Rousseau_-_adjusted.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rousseau was born on 28 June 1712 in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, a Protestant city-state and associate of the Swiss Confederacy that had been a Calvinist republic since 1536. His family belonged to the middle order of citizens with voting rights, and he generally signed his books Jean-Jacques Rousseau, Citizen of Geneva. In practice a small number of wealthy families ruled the city through its councils. When the reformer Pierre Fatio protested in 1707, he was shot by order of the Small Council, and Rousseau's grandfather, who had supported him, was penalised. His father, Isaac Rousseau, was a watchmaker, a former dance teacher and a lover of music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rousseau took formal music instruction at the house of Françoise-Louise de Warens, who housed him on and off for about 13 years. In 1742 he presented to the Académie des Sciences a system of musical notation using numbers, which they rejected while praising his effort. His first opera, Les Muses galantes, was written in 1743 and first performed in 1745. He wrote seven operas in all, the best known being the one-act The Village Soothsayer, composed in 1752. After 1752 he felt he could not go on working for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>A question set by the Academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> in 1750, whether the rebirth of the arts and sciences had purified morals, gave Rousseau what he called another universe. His essay in reply began his central argument that social and cultural progress had led to the moral degradation of humanity. In the Discourse on Inequality (1754) he held that private property is the source of social inequality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-social-contract">The Social Contract</a> and Émile appeared in 1762, and Julie, or the New Heloise in 1761. Émile's endorsement of religious toleration led to its condemnation in both Calvinist Geneva and Catholic Paris.</p><p>In The Social Contract Rousseau described a legitimate political order resting on the general will, the collective will of a society as a whole, reached through the active involvement of every citizen in discussion and voting. The phrase was not original, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> had used it. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> the Jacobin clubs invoked him in demanding equality, and Robespierre and Saint-Just took inspiration from him. His Confessions, completed in 1770, were published in 1782.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean-Jacques Rousseau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Enlightenment of Montesquieu, Voltaire, <b>Rousseau</b> and Diderot's Encyclopédie was largely conducted in French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century the image of free, virtuous Alpine republics, shaped by writers such as Albrecht von Haller and the Genevan <b>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</b>, influenced European political thought.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Plutarch's Life of Lycurgus influenced later political thinkers, including Machiavelli and <b>Rousseau</b>, and revolutionaries in eighteenth-century France cited Spartan austerity and civic virtue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…life, liberty and property and in the consent of the governed; Montesquieu urged the separation of legislative, executive and judicial power; and <b>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</b>, whose The Social Contract opens “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains”, placed sovereignty in the people and their general will.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</b>’s novel Julie, or the New Heloise of 1761 made feeling and natural scenery central to literature, and Edmund Burke’s Philosophical Enquiry of 1757 distinguished the beautiful from the sublime, the thrill of awe before storms, mountains and the infinite.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Laws (1748) that it is natural for a republic to have only a small territory, and <b>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</b>, who signed himself a citizen of Geneva, held in The Social Contract (1762) that a people could make its own laws only in a state small enough for…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. Died in Ermenonville, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.2,6.15&amp;year=1754" aria-label="Geneva on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4695 538 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jean-Jacques Rousseau"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5145 808h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5145 808h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.2,6.15&amp;year=1754">What was here in 1754? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Man is born free; and everywhere he is in chains. One thinks himself the master of others, and still remains a greater slave than they.&#8221;</p><footer>The Social Contract, book 1, chapter 1 &#183; tr. G. D. H. Cole, 1913 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46333/pg46333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The first man who, having enclosed a piece of ground, bethought himself of saying This is mine, and found people simple enough to believe him, was the real founder of civil society.&#8221;</p><footer>Discourse on the Origin of Inequality, part 2 &#183; tr. G. D. H. Cole, 1913 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46333/pg46333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;God makes all things good; man meddles with them and they become evil.&#8221;</p><footer>Emile, book 1 &#183; tr. Barbara Foxley, 1911 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5427/pg5427-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The strongest is never strong enough to be always the master, unless he transforms strength into right, and obedience into duty.&#8221;</p><footer>The Social Contract, book 1, chapter 3 &#183; tr. G. D. H. Cole, 1913 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46333/pg46333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…whoever refuses to obey the general will shall be compelled to do so by the whole body. This means nothing less than that he will be forced to be free…&#8221;</p><footer>The Social Contract, book 1, chapter 7 &#183; tr. G. D. H. Cole, 1913 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46333/pg46333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Each of us puts his person and all his power in common under the supreme direction of the general will, and, in our corporate capacity, we receive each member as an indivisible part of the whole.&#8221;</p><footer>The Social Contract, book 1, chapter 6 &#183; tr. G. D. H. Cole, 1913 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46333/pg46333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
